{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791149617,"nonce":"TLkpDa9fle6T9jZfAYzRtl2cujU=","message":"A concrete instance, not a flake this time: Coverage FAILED on da544c74 with\n\n  blinc_core::intern::tests::pool_size_is_deduped\n  assertion failed: pool_size() \u003c= before + 1\n\nwhich is exactly the exposure this issue describes. pool_size() reads a process-global intern pool. The test snapshots it, interns one key three times, and asserts the total grew by at most one. Under cargo test, which runs a binary's tests as threads, a sibling interning its own keys ('cn-button', 'foo', 'bar') moves that total between the two reads. nextest gives each test its own process, so the CI job never sees it.\n\nFixed by asserting the property instead of the proxy: the three handles are the same allocation, and the pool holds one entry for that key. No read of the shared total.\n\nHonest about the limits: I could not reproduce the race locally. Both the old and new assertions passed 40 runs at --test-threads=16 on macOS. The window is small and CI runs instrumented under llvm-cov on Linux, where everything is slower and the window wider. The mechanism is not in doubt, since CI named the assertion, but the local stress test proves nothing either way.\n\nThe earlier HANG recorded above was in the same crate, which is suggestive but still unproven.\n\nblinc_core is the crate most exposed here: it owns the global reactive graph, the global dirty flag and this intern pool, and its tests run as threads only in this one job.","files":null}]}